/* True or False minigame statement pool — common myths/misconceptions + surprising truths.
   Each item generated and adversarially fact-checked for accuracy. { q: statement, a: true|false, why: explanation, cat: category }.

   IT IS NOT ONLY HISTORY (Aug 2026, on request: "add more possible questions — they needn't only be about
   history, but any science"). The pool opened with five historical categories and now carries seven more —
   Physics, Chemistry, Astronomy, Biology, Earth science, Mathematics, Medicine — beside the original
   Science & invention. The category is shown to the reader, so it is the subject the statement is ABOUT
   rather than where it came from.
   The bar is the site's own and does not move for a wider subject: nothing here is a claim that "everyone
   knows", every `why` says what the evidence actually is, and a figure that is contested or has been
   revised says so rather than being rounded into certainty.

   PSYCHOLOGY IS THE THIRTEENTH CATEGORY (Aug 2026, on request), and it is the one that most needs the bar
   above rather than a relaxation of it: it is the subject with the largest stock of confidently repeated
   claims that were never established — learning styles, left- and right-brained people, the Mozart
   effect — and equally the one where a famous study is now read very differently from the
   way it was first reported. So a statement here says what was actually measured (Milgram's 26 of 40,
   the 54 per cent of a lie-detection meta-analysis), and where a celebrated experiment has not held up
   the `why` says so plainly rather than repeating the textbook version.
   TWO OF ITS THIRTEEN ARE ABOUT STUDYING ITSELF — the testing effect and the spacing effect — which is
   not a joke at the reader's expense: they are the two best-replicated findings in the whole category
   and they are what this site is built on, so a reader who meets them here is being told where the
   flashcards come from. */
window.TRUEFALSE = [{"q":"The Great Wall of China is the only human-made structure visible to the naked eye from the Moon.","a":false,"why":"This is a debunked myth: no individual human-made structure, including the Great Wall, is visible to the naked eye from the Moon (~384,000 km away), and the Wall is barely distinguishable even from low Earth orbit because it is narrow and blends into the surrounding terrain.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"The terracotta army guarding the tomb of Qin Shi Huang was discovered by farmers digging a well in 1974, not by archaeologists.","a":true,"why":"In March 1974, local farmers digging a well near Xi'an in Lintong District struck pottery fragments, an accidental find that brought in archaeologists who then excavated the buried army of thousands of life-sized clay soldiers built for Qin Shi Huang's mausoleum.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"The Great Wall as a single continuous barrier was mostly built by Qin Shi Huang in the 3rd century BCE and the brick wall tourists visit today is that original structure.","a":false,"why":"The iconic stone-and-brick wall tourists visit today, including the sections near Beijing such as Badaling and Mutianyu, was built or rebuilt during the Ming dynasty (1368–1644); the Qin-era walls under Qin Shi Huang were largely rammed earth, were not a single seamless continuous barrier, and have mostly eroded.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Confucius founded a religion that worshipped him as a god during his lifetime.","a":false,"why":"Confucius (551–479 BCE) was a teacher and philosopher whose ideas formed an ethical, social, and political tradition rather than a deity-centered religion he founded, and the cult that venerated him with sacrifices and near-divine honors arose centuries after his death, not during his lifetime.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Paper was invented in China centuries before it was used in Europe.","a":true,"why":"Paper was invented in China — hemp-fiber paper dates to at least the 2nd century BCE and the process was refined by Cai Lun around 105 CE — whereas papermaking only reached Europe via Islamic Spain and Italy in roughly the 11th–12th centuries CE, a gap of many centuries.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Gunpowder was first developed in China by alchemists who were searching for an elixir of immortality.","a":true,"why":"Gunpowder originated in Tang-dynasty China, where alchemists seeking an elixir of life accidentally combined saltpetre, charcoal, and sulfur into an incendiary mixture; the earliest formula appears in a 9th-century CE Taoist text.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Foot-binding was a custom practiced across all of China since the time of Confucius.","a":false,"why":"Foot-binding originated only in the Song dynasty (roughly the 10th–11th century CE), well over a millennium after Confucius (d. 479 BCE), and it was never universal — its prevalence varied by region, ethnicity, era, and social class (e.g., Manchus and many minority and Hakka groups did not practice it).","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Roman \"vomitoria\" were rooms where banquet guests would vomit so they could keep eating.","a":false,"why":"A vomitorium was an architectural passageway through which crowds entered and exited the seating tiers of a Roman amphitheater or theater (from Latin vomere, \"to disgorge\"); it was never a room for banquet guests to vomit in, which is a modern myth.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was built largely by paid Egyptian laborers, not by enslaved Hebrew slaves.","a":true,"why":"Excavations of the workers' settlement and tombs at Giza (by Lehner, Hawass, and others) show the Great Pyramid was built by an organized, provisioned, and compensated Egyptian workforce, not by enslaved Hebrews. The Hebrew-slave attribution has no archaeological or biblical support — even the Exodus narrative does not describe pyramid building.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Gladiator fights in the Colosseum almost always ended with the loser being killed.","a":false,"why":"Gladiators were costly to train, feed, and house, so killing them routinely was economically wasteful; defeated fighters were frequently granted reprieve (missio), and most matches did not end in death. Death rates in the high Empire are commonly estimated at roughly 1 in 5 to 1 in 10 bouts, not \"almost always.\"","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Roman concrete, used in structures like the Pantheon's dome, has in many cases proven more durable in seawater than much modern concrete.","a":true,"why":"Roman marine concrete used volcanic ash (pozzolana) with lime, and seawater percolating through it drove ongoing growth of minerals such as aluminous tobermorite that strengthened it over time; many Roman harbor structures have survived ~2,000 years, whereas modern Portland-cement concrete typically degrades in seawater.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Cleopatra lived closer in time to the moon landing than to the construction of the Great Pyramid of Giza.","a":true,"why":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was built around 2560 BCE and Cleopatra VII died in 30 BCE, a gap of roughly 2,500 years, whereas the 1969 moon landing came only about 2,000 years after her death — so she lived closer in time to the moon landing.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The Spartans famously inscribed the words \"Molon labe\" onto their shields as their standard military emblem.","a":false,"why":"\"Molon labe\" (\"come and take them\") is recorded by Plutarch as King Leonidas's spoken reply to the Persian demand to surrender weapons at Thermopylae, not a shield inscription. Spartan shields conventionally bore the letter lambda (Λ) for Lacedaemon as their emblem.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The ancient Olympic Games in Greece were held in honor of Zeus and barred married women from attending as spectators.","a":true,"why":"The ancient Olympic Games at Olympia were a religious festival dedicated to Zeus, whose great sanctuary and altar stood at the site. According to Pausanias, married women were barred from watching the games on pain of being thrown from Mount Typaion, while unmarried women and the priestess of Demeter Chamyne were exceptions.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The Colosseum was originally built and named in honor of the emperor Julius Caesar.","a":false,"why":"The Colosseum (the Flavian Amphitheater) was begun under Emperor Vespasian around 70-72 CE and inaugurated in 80 CE under his son Titus, more than a century after Julius Caesar's death in 44 BCE. Its formal name honors the Flavian dynasty, and the popular name \"Colosseum\" comes from the nearby Colossus of Nero, not Julius Caesar.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Vikings wore horned helmets into battle.","a":false,"why":"No horned battle helmet from the Viking Age has ever been found; surviving examples like the Gjermundbu helmet are simple rounded iron caps. The horned-Viking image is a 19th-century Romantic invention, popularized by costumes for Wagner's 1876 Ring cycle.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"Educated people in Columbus's time generally believed the Earth was flat.","a":false,"why":"The sphericity of the Earth was established in classical antiquity and remained standard knowledge among educated Europeans throughout the Middle Ages (taught, for example, in Sacrobosco's widely used De sphaera mundi). The objection to Columbus's plan concerned the size of the globe and the width of the ocean—he underestimated both—not the shape of the Earth.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"The accused in the Salem witch trials were executed by being burned at the stake.","a":false,"why":"The statement is FALSE. Of those executed in the 1692 Salem witch trials, nineteen were hanged and one man, Giles Corey, was pressed to death with stones; no one was burned at the stake, as colonial Massachusetts followed English law, under which burning was not the penalty for witchcraft.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"The Renaissance began in Italy.","a":true,"why":"The Renaissance is conventionally held to have begun in Italy, particularly in Florence and other Italian city-states, during the 14th century before spreading throughout Europe. Wealthy patrons such as the Medici family financed much of its art, scholarship, and architecture.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"Medieval Europeans commonly believed tomatoes and chili peppers were essential cooking ingredients.","a":false,"why":"Tomatoes and chili peppers are New World crops native to the Americas and were unknown in Europe until after Columbus's voyages of the late 15th century, after the medieval period; even once introduced, tomatoes were long regarded with suspicion rather than treated as essential.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"Christopher Columbus never set foot on the mainland of what is now the continental United States.","a":true,"why":"Across his four voyages (1492–1504), Columbus reached the Caribbean islands (Bahamas, Hispaniola, Cuba, Puerto Rico, etc.), the South American coast (1498, present-day Venezuela), and the Central American coast (1502), but never the mainland of the present-day continental United States.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"The medieval 'right of the first night,' giving a lord the right to sleep with a vassal's bride, is well documented as a widely practiced custom.","a":false,"why":"Modern historians regard the \"right of the first night\" (droit du seigneur / jus primae noctis) as a myth and literary motif rather than a documented, widely practiced legal custom; there is no solid evidence it was ever an actual exercised right in medieval Europe.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"Universities such as Bologna, Oxford, and Paris were founded during the Middle Ages.","a":true,"why":"The Universities of Bologna (c. 1088), Oxford (teaching by c. 1096, organized c. 1167), and Paris (c. 1150) all originated in the High Middle Ages, and the university as an institution of higher learning is itself a medieval European creation.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"Napoleon Bonaparte was unusually short for his time, standing only about five feet two inches tall.","a":false,"why":"Napoleon stood roughly 5 feet 6–7 inches (about 1.68–1.70 m), average or slightly above for a Frenchman of his day; the \"short\" myth comes from confusing the French inch (pouce) with the English inch and from British propaganda caricatures.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"The guillotine was still being used to carry out executions in France within living memory of the moon landings.","a":true,"why":"France's last guillotine execution (Hamida Djandoubi) took place on 10 September 1977 — eight years after the July 1969 Apollo 11 moon landing — and the death penalty was not abolished in France until 1981, so the guillotine was indeed in use well within living memory of the moon landings.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"During the storming of the Bastille in 1789, the revolutionaries freed thousands of political prisoners held in the fortress.","a":false,"why":"When the Bastille was stormed on 14 July 1789 it held only seven prisoners (four forgers, two mental patients, and one aristocrat), none of them notable political prisoners; the assault was largely symbolic and aimed chiefly at seizing the gunpowder and arms stored there.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"World War I formally ended with the armistice that took effect at the eleventh hour of November 11, 1918.","a":false,"why":"The November 11, 1918 armistice was only a ceasefire that halted the fighting; World War I was formally and legally ended by the postwar peace treaties, chiefly the Treaty of Versailles signed on June 28, 1919 (with parallel treaties ending the war with the other Central Powers).","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Adolf Hitler became Germany's leader by winning an outright majority of the popular vote in a free national election.","a":false,"why":"The Nazis never won an outright majority of the popular vote in a free national election; Hitler was appointed Chancellor in January 1933 by President Hindenburg and then consolidated dictatorial power through legislation (notably the Enabling Act) and intimidation.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"The famous gunfight associated with Tombstone, Arizona did not actually take place inside the O.K. Corral.","a":true,"why":"The October 26, 1881 gunfight took place in a narrow vacant lot on Fremont Street (near Fly's boarding house), not inside the O.K. Corral, whose entrances were several doors away; the \"O.K. Corral\" name became attached to the event later, largely through popular retellings and film.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Albert Einstein's theory of general relativity was first confirmed by observations of a solar eclipse in 1919.","a":true,"why":"Arthur Eddington's two expeditions during the total solar eclipse of 29 May 1919 (to Príncipe and Sobral, Brazil) measured the Sun's gravity bending starlight by an amount matching Einstein's general-relativity prediction; the results, announced in November 1919, were the first major confirmation of the theory and made Einstein world-famous.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Thomas Edison invented the first electric light bulb.","a":false,"why":"Working incandescent and arc lamps were built by Humphry Davy, Warren de la Rue, Joseph Swan, and others decades before Edison; Edison's 1879 contribution was the first commercially practical, long-lasting bulb and its surrounding power-distribution system, not the invention of the electric light bulb.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Albert Einstein failed mathematics as a schoolboy.","a":false,"why":"Einstein excelled at mathematics as a youth, teaching himself calculus by about age 15; the \"failed math\" claim is a famous myth, which Einstein himself debunked by noting he had mastered differential and integral calculus before fifteen.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Isaac Newton served as Master of the Royal Mint and had counterfeiters prosecuted and executed.","a":true,"why":"Isaac Newton was appointed Warden of the Royal Mint in 1696 and became Master of the Mint in 1699; in his role pursuing counterfeiters, he gathered evidence that led to the conviction and execution of forgers, most famously William Chaloner, who was hanged in 1699.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Benjamin Franklin proved lightning was electrical by flying a kite that was struck by a lightning bolt.","a":false,"why":"Franklin's 1752 kite experiment detected ambient electrical charge from a storm cloud (sparks drawn from a key on the wet string), not a direct lightning bolt hitting the kite; a direct strike would likely have been fatal, as it was for Georg Richmann in 1753.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Nikola Tesla's alternating-current system won the \"War of the Currents\" over Edison's direct-current system as the standard for power distribution.","a":true,"why":"In the late-1880s/1890s \"War of the Currents,\" the alternating-current system promoted by George Westinghouse using Tesla's polyphase patents prevailed over Edison's direct current because AC could be stepped up to high voltage for efficient long-distance transmission, becoming the standard for power distribution.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Charles Darwin coined the phrase \"survival of the fittest\" in On the Origin of Species.","a":false,"why":"The phrase \"survival of the fittest\" was coined by Herbert Spencer in his 1864 Principles of Biology after reading Darwin; Darwin did not use it in the original On the Origin of Species (1859), only adopting it from the 5th edition (1869) onward.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"The mathematician who wrote the first published algorithm intended for a machine, often called the first computer programmer, was a woman.","a":true,"why":"Ada Lovelace's 1843 notes on Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine included an algorithm to compute Bernoulli numbers, widely regarded as the first published algorithm intended to be carried out by a machine, earning her recognition as the first computer programmer.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"The imperial civil-service examination, choosing officials by written tests of merit, existed in China roughly a thousand years before any comparable system in Europe.","a":true,"why":"China's exam system began under the Sui dynasty around 605 CE and became the main route to office under the Tang and Song. Britain only introduced competitive civil-service exams in the 19th century, explicitly inspired by the Chinese model.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Wu Zetian, who declared her own Zhou dynasty in 690 CE, was the only woman in Chinese history to rule as emperor in her own right rather than as a regent or consort.","a":true,"why":"Wu Zetian took the title huangdi (emperor) and ruled as sole sovereign from 690 to 705 CE. Other powerful women such as Empress Dowager Cixi wielded power behind the throne, but Wu is the only woman ever to hold the imperial title itself.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"The magnetic compass was used in China for feng shui and divination for centuries before anyone thought to use it for navigation at sea.","a":true,"why":"The earliest Chinese 'south-pointing' lodestone devices, dating to the Han dynasty and earlier, were used to align buildings and tombs and for geomancy. The compass was only adapted for maritime navigation around the Song dynasty, roughly 800 years later.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Fortune cookies, served in American Chinese restaurants, actually originated in China and were brought over by early immigrants.","a":false,"why":"Fortune cookies derive from Japanese tsujiura senbei crackers and were popularized in California by Japanese immigrants in the early 20th century. Production shifted to Chinese-American businesses during World War II, which is how the cookie became linked to Chinese restaurants.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Soil tests around the still-sealed tomb of China's first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, have found mercury concentrations far above natural levels, matching an ancient text describing rivers of liquid mercury inside.","a":true,"why":"The historian Sima Qian wrote that the tomb contained rivers and seas of flowing mercury representing the empire. Modern surveys since the 1980s have detected mercury up to about 100 times background levels in a pattern matching that description, and the central burial chamber remains unexcavated.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"The world's first government-issued paper money was printed in China during the Song dynasty, centuries before banknotes appeared in Europe.","a":true,"why":"The jiaozi, first issued by merchants in Chengdu, was taken over by the Song government in the 1020s, making it the first state-issued paper currency. Paper money would not appear at any notable scale in the West until roughly five centuries later.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Dishes like chop suey and General Tso's chicken that anchor American Chinese menus are traditional recipes brought directly from China.","a":false,"why":"Chop suey is an American invention, and General Tso's chicken was created in Taiwan in 1955 before being heavily sweetened and battered in New York. Neither reflects traditional Chinese home cooking and both were tailored to American tastes.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"In the early 1400s Admiral Zheng He commanded a Ming fleet whose largest ships and sheer numbers dwarfed anything Christopher Columbus would later sail.","a":true,"why":"Zheng He's expeditions involved fleets of around 200 vessels and roughly 27,000 men, compared with Columbus's three small ships. Even conservative estimates place his largest treasure ships among the biggest wooden vessels ever built, far larger than the Santa Maria.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Confucius was a celebrated and influential figure across China during his own lifetime.","a":false,"why":"Confucius (551-479 BCE) held only minor offices, spent years wandering between states seeking a ruler who would adopt his ideas, and died believing he had failed. His teachings became China's dominant state philosophy only generations later, from the Han dynasty onward.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Bi Sheng invented movable-type printing in China around the 1040s, roughly four centuries before Johannes Gutenberg built his press in Europe.","a":true,"why":"During the Song dynasty Bi Sheng created movable type from baked clay characters, as recorded by the scholar Shen Kuo. Gutenberg developed his metal movable-type press in Europe around 1439, about 400 years later.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"At its height under the Tang dynasty, the Chinese capital Chang'an was the most populous city in the world, holding around a million people.","a":true,"why":"In the mid-8th century Chang'an (modern Xi'an) had roughly one million inhabitants within its walls and was a cosmopolitan hub at the end of the Silk Road. It is generally reckoned the largest and most populous city on Earth during much of the Tang era.","cat":"Chinese history"},{"q":"Cesarean sections are named after Julius Caesar because he was delivered by one, which is why his mother did not survive the birth.","a":false,"why":"Caesar's mother Aurelia lived for decades after his birth, whereas in Roman times the operation was performed only on dead or dying women. The name more likely derives from the Latin caedere ('to cut') or an ancestor, not from Julius Caesar himself.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Ancient Greek marble statues and temples, which we picture as pure white, were originally painted in bright, vivid colors.","a":true,"why":"Traces of pigment detected under ultraviolet and raking light show that Greek sculpture and architecture were richly painted in reds, blues, greens and gold. The bare-white look is an accident of weathering that stripped the paint over the centuries.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Cleopatra, the last ruler of Egypt's Ptolemaic dynasty, was descended from Macedonian Greeks rather than being ethnically native Egyptian.","a":true,"why":"The Ptolemies descended from Ptolemy I, a Macedonian Greek general of Alexander the Great, and largely intermarried to keep the line Hellenic. Cleopatra VII is even noted as the first of the dynasty to learn the Egyptian language after some 300 years of rule.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The idea that a Roman crowd gave a defeated gladiator 'thumbs down' to signal his death is a well-documented fact from ancient sources.","a":false,"why":"The Latin phrase pollice verso is genuine but its exact gesture and meaning are unknown, and scholars debate whether the thumb went up, down, sideways or was hidden. The familiar thumbs-down image largely comes from Jean-Leon Gerome's 1872 painting, not from Roman evidence.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Archaeological evidence indicates Roman gladiators ate a largely plant-based, grain-heavy diet and were nicknamed 'barley-eaters.'","a":true,"why":"Bone-isotope analysis of gladiators buried at Ephesus showed a diet dominated by wheat, barley and beans with little meat. This matches the ancient nickname hordearii, or 'barley men,' recorded for gladiators.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The Babylonians understood the relationship later called the Pythagorean theorem more than a thousand years before Pythagoras was born.","a":true,"why":"The clay tablet Plimpton 322 and other Old Babylonian texts, dating to around 1800 BCE, list Pythagorean triples and apply the right-triangle rule. That is roughly a millennium before Pythagoras lived in the 6th century BCE.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Emperor Nero famously played a fiddle while watching Rome burn in 64 CE.","a":false,"why":"The fiddle did not exist in Roman times, as bowed string instruments only appeared around the 11th century. Nero was reportedly not even in Rome when the fire began, and the tale of him playing music emerged in later European retellings.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The Great Library of Alexandria was destroyed in a single catastrophic fire that wiped out ancient knowledge overnight.","a":false,"why":"Historians now see the library's loss as a gradual decline over centuries, driven by funding cuts, neglect, political instability and Alexandria's fading status as a scholarly center. Fires were only one factor among many in its slow disappearance.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Under Emperor Titus, Rome's Colosseum was flooded so that mock naval battles with real ships could be staged inside it.","a":true,"why":"Ancient sources describe naumachiae, or staged sea battles, held in the newly opened Colosseum around 80 CE. This was possible only in its first years, before the underground hypogeum was built beneath the arena floor, making flooding impractical.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The Emperor Constantine made Christianity the official state religion of the Roman Empire with his Edict of Milan in 313 CE.","a":false,"why":"The Edict of Milan only granted religious toleration and ended the persecution of Christians. Christianity became the empire's official state religion later, under Emperor Theodosius I with the Edict of Thessalonica in 380 CE.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The Mauryan emperor Ashoka renounced war and embraced Buddhism after being horrified by the slaughter he caused in his conquest of Kalinga.","a":true,"why":"By his own edicts, Ashoka felt profound remorse over the Kalinga war around 260 BCE, in which he says over a hundred thousand were killed or deported. He then turned to Buddhist principles and promoted 'dhamma,' or righteous conduct, across his empire.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"The secret of silk production, guarded in China on pain of death, was smuggled to the Byzantine Empire by monks who hid silkworm eggs in hollow canes.","a":true,"why":"The 6th-century historian Procopius records that two monks concealed silkworm eggs in hollow bamboo staves and carried them to Constantinople for Emperor Justinian around 552 CE. This broke China's long monopoly and launched a Byzantine silk industry.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Medieval knights in full plate armor were so weighed down that they had to be hoisted onto their horses with a crane.","a":false,"why":"A full suit of field armor weighed only about 20 to 25 kg, distributed over the body, so knights could mount, run, and even do handsprings in it. The crane story appears to be a later joke, traceable to an 1843 issue of Punch magazine, and was cemented by films like Laurence Olivier's Henry V (1944).","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"Making prisoners 'walk the plank' was a routine execution method among real pirates.","a":false,"why":"Historical records document only a tiny handful of genuine plank-walking incidents; real pirates usually just threw victims overboard, marooned them, or flogged them. The dramatic image was popularized by 19th-century writers such as Robert Louis Stevenson and then by Hollywood.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"The University of Oxford was already teaching students before the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan was founded.","a":true,"why":"Teaching is documented at Oxford by 1096, and the university grew rapidly after 1167. The Aztec capital Tenochtitlan was not founded until 1325, more than two centuries later.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"The Mongol Empire was the largest contiguous land empire in all of history.","a":true,"why":"At its height in the 13th century the Mongol Empire covered roughly 24 million square kilometers, stretching from Korea and China to Eastern Europe. No other single connected land empire has ever matched that reach.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"The official records of the 1666 Great Fire of London list only a handful of deaths.","a":true,"why":"Contemporary records name only about six victims, even though the fire destroyed some 13,000 houses and left much of the city in ashes. Many historians suspect the true toll was higher, since the intense heat could have cremated bodies and the deaths of the poor often went unrecorded.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"Marie Antoinette responded to news that peasants had no bread by saying 'Let them eat cake.'","a":false,"why":"There is no evidence she ever said it, and the phrase predates her prominence. Jean-Jacques Rousseau attributed a similar line about brioche to an unnamed 'great princess' in his Confessions, written around 1765 when Marie Antoinette was a nine-year-old child in Austria.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Napoleon's soldiers shot off the nose of the Great Sphinx with cannon fire during his 1798 Egyptian campaign.","a":false,"why":"Drawings by the Danish traveler Frederic Louis Norden, made around 1737 and published in 1755, already show the Sphinx without its nose, decades before Napoleon arrived. Chisel marks on the face suggest it was deliberately pried off much earlier, with a 15th-century account blaming a Sufi iconoclast.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Catherine the Great of Russia died in a bizarre accident while attempting to have sex with a horse.","a":false,"why":"Catherine suffered a stroke in her palace and died in bed the following day, on 17 November 1796, at age 67. The horse story was invented by her enemies, likely in the French aristocracy, to smear a powerful woman who had seized and expanded an empire.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Betsy Ross is the woman who is historically documented to have sewn the very first American flag.","a":false,"why":"The story rests entirely on claims made in 1870 by Ross's grandson, roughly a century after the events, with no contemporary letters, receipts, or records to support it. Historians often point instead to Francis Hopkinson, a signer of the Declaration of Independence, as a likelier designer of an early flag.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Rosa Parks was the first Black passenger in Montgomery, Alabama, to be arrested for refusing to give up her bus seat.","a":false,"why":"Fifteen-year-old Claudette Colvin was arrested for the same act on 2 March 1955, nine months before Parks. Civil rights leaders chose Parks, an older, employed activist, as the public face of the ensuing bus boycott and test case.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"The original Coca-Cola sold in the 1880s and 1890s actually contained cocaine.","a":true,"why":"Coca-Cola's early formula used extract of coca leaf and contained small amounts of cocaine, alongside caffeine from the kola nut. The company began removing the cocaine around 1903 and switched to 'spent' decocainized leaves, becoming essentially cocaine-free by the late 1920s.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"George Washington's doctors drained roughly 40 percent of his blood in his final hours as a medical treatment.","a":true,"why":"When Washington fell gravely ill in December 1799, his physicians used bloodletting, then a standard remedy, removing an estimated 3.5 to 5 pints over several sessions. He died on the evening of 14 December, and modern doctors think the heavy blood loss likely hastened his death.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"In 1932 the Australian army was deployed with machine guns against wild emus and largely failed to defeat them.","a":true,"why":"During the so-called Emu War in Western Australia, soldiers with two machine guns were sent to cull thousands of emus damaging crops, but the birds scattered and proved nearly impossible to hit. After weeks only a few hundred to about a thousand were killed, and the campaign was mocked as a defeat.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"The armored fighting vehicle called the 'tank' got its name from a wartime cover story that the vehicles were mobile water tanks.","a":true,"why":"To hide the new weapon's purpose during World War I, the British disguised the vehicles as water carriers, and factory workers called them 'tanks' after the steel water tanks they resembled. The name was officially adopted in December 1915 and stuck.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"Thomas Crapper, whose name is linked with toilets, actually invented the flush toilet.","a":false,"why":"Crapper was a real Victorian sanitary engineer who promoted and improved plumbing, but he did not invent the flush toilet. A flushing water closet was built by Sir John Harington in 1596, and the flush mechanism was patented by Alexander Cumming in 1775, long before Crapper's career.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"The word 'bug' for a technical glitch was coined in 1947 when a real moth was found inside the Harvard Mark II computer.","a":false,"why":"The 1947 moth was real and famously taped into a logbook, but the note itself reads 'first actual case of bug being found,' showing 'bug' was already common slang for a fault. Engineers had used 'bug' that way for decades, and Thomas Edison wrote of 'bugs' in his inventions as early as 1878.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Surgeons were using ether to put patients to sleep for operations before they understood the need for antiseptic, germ-free surgery.","a":true,"why":"Ether anesthesia was publicly demonstrated by William Morton at Massachusetts General Hospital in October 1846. Joseph Lister's antiseptic techniques only arrived about two decades later, with his first antiseptic operation in 1865, so for years surgery was painless but still often deadly from infection.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Although penicillin was discovered in 1928, it was not turned into a mass-produced, life-saving drug until the 1940s.","a":true,"why":"Alexander Fleming noticed penicillin's antibacterial effect in 1928 but could not purify it into a usable medicine and largely set it aside. It took Howard Florey, Ernst Chain, and their Oxford team around 1940, plus wartime American industrial fermentation, to mass-produce it in time to save many WWII casualties.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Alexander Graham Bell and a rival inventor filed telephone patent paperwork at the very same patent office on the same day.","a":true,"why":"On 14 February 1876 both Bell and Elisha Gray submitted telephone-related filings to the U.S. Patent Office, with Bell's arriving first that day. Bell was granted the patent weeks later, and the timing fed a long-running dispute over who truly invented the telephone.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"Nothing with mass can be accelerated to the speed of light in a vacuum.","a":true,"why":"Special relativity makes the energy needed to accelerate a massive object rise without limit as it approaches light speed, so it can be pushed arbitrarily close to that speed but never reach it. Light itself travels slower through glass or water, and particles can outrun it there — which is what causes Cherenkov radiation — but nothing outruns light in a vacuum.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"A penny dropped from the top of a skyscraper would kill someone on the pavement below.","a":false,"why":"A penny is light and flat, so air resistance caps its fall at roughly 11 metres per second (25 miles per hour) and it tumbles rather than falling point-first. It would sting on impact but carries nowhere near enough energy to break skin, let alone kill.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"Glass is a very slow-moving liquid, which is why old cathedral windows are thicker at the bottom.","a":false,"why":"Glass is an amorphous solid, and at room temperature its flow is so slight that a medieval pane would not visibly sag over billions of years. Old windows vary in thickness because crown glass was spun into uneven discs, and glaziers usually set the heavier edge downwards.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"Pure water is a poor conductor of electricity.","a":true,"why":"Water molecules themselves barely carry current: pure water conducts about a ten-millionth as well as seawater. What makes tap water, sweat and river water dangerous is the dissolved salts and minerals, whose ions do the conducting.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Electric current travels through a wire at close to the speed of light, yet the electrons themselves creep along at less than a millimetre per second.","a":true,"why":"The signal is an electromagnetic wave that propagates through and around the conductor at a large fraction of light speed, which is why a lamp lights the instant the switch closes. The electrons drift much more slowly, typically well under a millimetre per second in household wiring.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"The Coriolis effect decides which way water spins as it drains from a bath.","a":false,"why":"The Coriolis force is far too weak at the scale of a basin: the shape of the bowl, the position of the drain and any residual swirl from filling completely overwhelm it. It does govern the rotation of large weather systems, which turn anticlockwise in the northern hemisphere and clockwise in the southern.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"Lightning never strikes the same place twice.","a":false,"why":"Tall, isolated, conductive structures are struck repeatedly: the Empire State Building takes roughly twenty to twenty-five strikes a year. Lightning follows the easiest path to ground, and a good path stays a good path.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"Sound travels faster through water than through air.","a":true,"why":"Sound moves at about 1,480 metres per second in seawater against roughly 343 metres per second in air at room temperature. Denser, stiffer media transmit the pressure wave more readily, which is why whale song carries for hundreds of kilometres.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"Absolute zero is −273.15 degrees Celsius.","a":true,"why":"Absolute zero is the point at which a system has the least possible thermal energy, and it defines the bottom of the Kelvin scale at 0 K. It has never been reached: laboratories have come within a few billionths of a degree of it but cannot remove the last of the energy.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"Seen from space, the Sun is white rather than yellow.","a":true,"why":"The Sun emits across the visible spectrum with no strong peak in yellow, so above the atmosphere it looks white. It appears yellow, orange or red from the ground because air scatters the shorter blue wavelengths away, an effect that grows stronger the lower the Sun sits.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, so it is also the hottest.","a":false,"why":"Venus is much hotter, with a surface around 465 degrees Celsius (869 Fahrenheit) against Mercury's peak of about 430. Venus's dense carbon-dioxide atmosphere traps heat so effectively that its surface stays at that temperature day and night, while Mercury, which has almost no atmosphere, plunges to about −180 degrees at night.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Astronauts float aboard the International Space Station because there is no gravity at that altitude.","a":false,"why":"Earth's gravity at the station's altitude is still about 90 per cent of its strength at the ground. The station and everything in it are in continuous free fall around the planet, moving sideways fast enough to keep missing it, and it is that shared fall — not an absence of gravity — that produces weightlessness.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"A day on Venus lasts longer than a year on Venus.","a":true,"why":"Venus turns on its axis once every 243 Earth days while completing an orbit in about 225, so its sidereal day outlasts its year. It also rotates backwards compared with almost every other planet, so the Sun there rises in the west.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"The Moon keeps the same face towards Earth because it does not rotate.","a":false,"why":"The Moon rotates once on its axis in exactly the time it takes to orbit Earth, a state called tidal locking, and it is that match rather than an absence of spin that keeps one hemisphere turned towards us. Tidal friction over billions of years slowed its rotation into step with its orbit.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Saturn is the only planet in the solar system with rings.","a":false,"why":"Jupiter, Uranus and Neptune all have ring systems, discovered between 1977 and 1989. Saturn's are simply far brighter and wider, being made largely of reflective water ice, while the others are dark and thin.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach Earth.","a":true,"why":"Light covers the average 150 million kilometres (93 million miles) from Sun to Earth in roughly eight minutes and twenty seconds. The Sun we see is therefore always the Sun as it was eight minutes ago.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"The asteroid belt is so densely packed that a spacecraft has to steer carefully to avoid collisions.","a":false,"why":"The belt holds a great many objects but spreads them across an enormous volume, leaving average separations of hundreds of thousands of kilometres. Every probe sent through it — Pioneer, Voyager, Galileo, Cassini, Juno — crossed without needing to dodge anything.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Polaris, the North Star, is the brightest star in the night sky.","a":false,"why":"Sirius is much the brightest; Polaris ranks somewhere around fiftieth. Its usefulness comes from its position almost directly above Earth's north pole, which keeps it nearly still while the rest of the sky wheels around it.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Human beings have exactly five senses.","a":false,"why":"Sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch leave out several others that are just as real, including balance, the sense of where your limbs are without looking, and separate systems for temperature and pain. Counts vary with definition, but every modern list runs well past five.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"People use only ten per cent of their brains.","a":false,"why":"Brain imaging shows activity across virtually the whole organ over the course of a day, and damage to almost any region causes some deficit. The brain uses about a fifth of the body's energy at rest, which would be an extraordinary waste if nine-tenths of it did nothing.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Goldfish have a memory lasting only about three seconds.","a":false,"why":"Goldfish have been trained to push levers, run mazes and recognise feeding times, retaining what they learned for months. The three-second claim has no experimental basis at all.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"A banana is botanically a berry, while a strawberry is not.","a":true,"why":"A berry is a fruit that develops from a single ovary with its seeds inside the flesh, which describes bananas, grapes and tomatoes. A strawberry is an accessory fruit: the fleshy part grows from the flower's receptacle, and the true fruits are the pips dotted over its surface.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"A human body contains at least as many bacterial cells as human cells.","a":true,"why":"The best current estimate puts a typical adult at roughly 38 trillion bacteria against about 30 trillion human cells, most of the bacteria in the large intestine. That figure revised a much-repeated ten-to-one ratio, which turned out to rest on a rough calculation from 1972.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Blood in the veins is blue until it meets the air.","a":false,"why":"Blood is red at every point in the circulation: bright scarlet when carrying oxygen, dark crimson when it has given it up. Veins look blue through the skin because tissue scatters and absorbs light unevenly, favouring the blue wavelengths that reach the eye.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Different regions of the tongue detect different tastes — sweet at the tip, bitter at the back.","a":false,"why":"Every taste can be detected across the whole tongue. The famous map came from a mistranslated German paper of 1901 that reported only slight regional differences in sensitivity, which an American textbook redrew as sharply bounded zones.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"An octopus has three hearts and blue blood.","a":true,"why":"Two hearts pump blood through the gills and a third drives it around the rest of the body. The blood is blue because octopuses carry oxygen using haemocyanin, which is copper-based, rather than the iron-based haemoglobin that makes vertebrate blood red.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Sharks do not get cancer.","a":false,"why":"Tumours have been documented in dozens of shark species, including malignant ones. The claim was popularised in the 1990s to sell shark cartilage supplements, which trials have since found ineffective against cancer.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Bats are blind.","a":false,"why":"Every one of the roughly 1,400 bat species can see, and the large fruit bats rely mainly on vision rather than echolocation. Echolocating bats use sound in the dark in addition to sight, not instead of it.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Tardigrades have survived direct exposure to the vacuum of space.","a":true,"why":"In a 2007 European Space Agency experiment, dried tardigrades were exposed to open space in low Earth orbit for ten days and revived on return, with some going on to lay viable eggs. They survive by entering a desiccated dormant state called a tun.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Fingernails and hair go on growing for a while after death.","a":false,"why":"Growth stops when the cells that produce nail and hair lose their blood supply. The appearance comes from the skin drying and retracting, which exposes more of a nail or hair shaft than was visible in life.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Honey can remain edible for thousands of years.","a":true,"why":"Honey holds so little free water and is acidic enough that bacteria and moulds cannot grow in it, and bee enzymes add a trace of hydrogen peroxide. Sealed jars recovered from Egyptian tombs have been reported as still unspoiled.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Diamond and graphite are both made of nothing but carbon.","a":true,"why":"They are the same element arranged differently: diamond bonds each carbon to four neighbours in a rigid three-dimensional lattice, while graphite stacks flat sheets that slide over one another. That difference alone makes one the hardest natural material and the other soft enough to write with.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Helium was found on the Sun before it was found on Earth.","a":true,"why":"An unexplained yellow line in the spectrum of the 1868 solar eclipse was attributed to a new element, named after Helios, the Greek sun god. It was not isolated on Earth until 1895, when William Ramsay extracted it from a uranium mineral.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Adding salt to a pan of water makes it boil sooner.","a":false,"why":"Dissolved salt raises the boiling point slightly, so salted water has to get hotter before it boils. The quantities used in cooking change the temperature by well under a degree, so in practice the effect on timing is negligible — and it works against speed, not for it.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Stainless steel resists rust because chromium in the alloy forms an invisible protective film.","a":true,"why":"Chromium at above roughly a tenth of the alloy reacts with oxygen to form a dense chromium-oxide layer only a few atoms thick. It seals the metal beneath and re-forms wherever the surface is scratched, which is why a scrape does not start a rust patch.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Mount Everest is the tallest mountain on Earth when measured from its base.","a":false,"why":"Everest has the highest summit above sea level, at 8,849 metres (29,032 feet), but it rises from a plateau already several kilometres high. Mauna Kea in Hawaii stands about 10,200 metres (33,500 feet) from its base on the sea floor, of which only the top 4,200 are above water.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"Earth is closest to the Sun in July.","a":false,"why":"Earth reaches perihelion, its closest approach, in early January, and is farthest away in early July. The seasons are caused by the tilt of Earth's axis, not by its distance from the Sun, which varies by only about three per cent.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"A lightning bolt is hotter than the surface of the Sun.","a":true,"why":"The air in a lightning channel is heated to something like 30,000 kelvin, against roughly 5,800 kelvin at the Sun's visible surface. It lasts only microseconds, which is why the surrounding air cools again almost at once — and why the sudden expansion makes thunder.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"The Sahara is the largest desert on Earth.","a":false,"why":"A desert is defined by how little precipitation it receives rather than by heat, and by that measure Antarctica is the largest at roughly 14 million square kilometres (5.4 million square miles). The Sahara, at about 9.2 million, is the largest hot desert.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"Diamonds are formed from coal squeezed under pressure.","a":false,"why":"Almost all natural diamonds crystallised in the mantle more than 150 kilometres down, and most are over a billion years old — far older than the land plants that coal is made from. Their carbon came from deep within Earth, and volcanic pipes carried them up.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"The shore of the Dead Sea is the lowest exposed land on Earth.","a":true,"why":"Its surface lies more than 430 metres (1,410 feet) below sea level and has been falling as the lake shrinks. Lower points exist only beneath ice or water, such as the bedrock trenches under the Antarctic ice sheet.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"There are more real numbers between 0 and 1 than there are whole numbers altogether.","a":true,"why":"Georg Cantor showed in 1874 that the whole numbers can be listed one after another while the reals in any interval cannot: his diagonal argument constructs a number missing from any proposed list. The reals are therefore a strictly larger infinity than the integers.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"The exact value of pi has been calculated in full.","a":false,"why":"Pi is irrational, proved by Johann Lambert in 1761, so its decimal expansion neither ends nor repeats and no finite calculation can complete it. Trillions of digits have been computed, but a few dozen are enough to place a circle the size of the observable universe to within an atom's width.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"In a room of 23 people, the odds are better than even that two of them share a birthday.","a":true,"why":"The comparison is not between one person and the rest but between all 253 possible pairs, which makes a match far likelier than intuition suggests. With 23 people the probability is about 50.7 per cent, and with 70 it is over 99.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"Cracking your knuckles causes arthritis.","a":false,"why":"The sound is a gas bubble forming in the joint fluid as the surfaces are pulled apart, and studies comparing habitual crackers with non-crackers find no difference in arthritis rates. One doctor cracked the knuckles of a single hand for over sixty years and found no difference between his hands.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"Most body heat is lost through the head.","a":false,"why":"Heat leaves roughly in proportion to the area of skin exposed, and the head is about a tenth of the body's surface. The idea came from a military experiment in which subjects were dressed in survival suits with only their heads uncovered.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"Antibiotics do nothing against the viruses that cause colds and flu.","a":true,"why":"Antibiotics work on structures and processes peculiar to bacteria, such as the cell wall, which viruses do not have. Taking them for a viral infection cannot help and adds to the resistance that makes them less effective when they are genuinely needed.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"Taking vitamin C stops you catching a cold.","a":false,"why":"Reviews of dozens of controlled trials find that regular supplementation does not reduce how often people in the general population catch colds. It shortens their duration slightly, and does reduce incidence in one narrow group: people under sustained extreme physical stress, such as marathon runners and soldiers on subarctic exercises.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"The appendix is a useless leftover with no known function.","a":false,"why":"The appendix is rich in lymphoid tissue and is now thought to act as a reservoir of beneficial gut bacteria, helping to repopulate the intestine after an illness has flushed it out. People manage perfectly well without one, but that is not the same as its doing nothing.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"A microwave oven cooks food from the inside out.","a":false,"why":"Microwaves penetrate only a couple of centimetres before their energy is absorbed, so they heat the outer layer and the middle warms by conduction, much as in a conventional oven. Food can seem to cook from within because the dry surface loses heat to the air while the moist interior does not.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"The first published computer program was written by a woman.","a":true,"why":"Ada Lovelace's 1843 notes on Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine include a step-by-step method for computing Bernoulli numbers on a machine that was never built. She also saw further than Babbage in arguing that such a machine could manipulate symbols of any kind, not only numbers.","cat":"Science & invention"},{"q":"People are either left-brained and logical or right-brained and creative.","a":false,"why":"Some functions are lateralised — language usually sits mostly on the left — but there is no evidence of whole-brain 'types'. A 2013 study that mapped resting-state connections in more than a thousand brains found no individuals with a left- or right-dominant network.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"Listening to Mozart has been shown to make children more intelligent.","a":false,"why":"The 1993 study behind the 'Mozart effect' tested college students, measured one spatial-reasoning task, and found a small improvement that faded within about fifteen minutes. The claim about children's intelligence was added by others afterwards, and later attempts to reproduce even the original effect have been mixed.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"In Stanley Milgram's obedience experiments most volunteers went on delivering what they believed were dangerous electric shocks when an experimenter told them to.","a":true,"why":"In the best-known 1961 version, 26 of 40 participants — about two thirds — continued to the maximum setting on the shock generator. Later analysis of Milgram's own archive shows the experimenter improvised more prompting than the published account suggested, but the headline rate is not in dispute.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"Once a memory has been stored, recalling it leaves it unchanged, like replaying a recording.","a":false,"why":"Remembering is reconstruction rather than playback. Elizabeth Loftus's misinformation experiments showed that details supplied after an event are readily absorbed into the memory of it, and research on reconsolidation finds that a recalled memory becomes briefly open to alteration.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"People can reliably spot a liar from body language at well above chance.","a":false,"why":"A meta-analysis of some two hundred studies put average accuracy at about 54 per cent, barely better than a coin toss, and found police officers and other supposed experts no better than students. The confident cues people rely on, such as looking away, are not associated with lying.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"The murder that gave psychology the 'bystander effect' was reported with details that later turned out to be substantially wrong.","a":true,"why":"The 1964 killing of Kitty Genovese in New York was reported as having been watched by 38 witnesses who did nothing, a figure the newspaper account cannot support: several people did not see the attack, at least two called the police, and a neighbour went to her. The effect itself has since been demonstrated in controlled experiments.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"Opposites attract: people tend to pair up with partners unlike themselves.","a":false,"why":"The consistent finding is the reverse. Couples resemble each other above chance in education, political and religious attitudes, age and habits — a pattern called assortative mating — and similarity of attitudes is one of the better-established predictors of attraction.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"Teaching pupils in their own 'learning style' — visual, auditory or kinaesthetic — measurably improves how much they learn.","a":false,"why":"People readily report a preferred style, but the experiments that matter test whether matching instruction to it helps, and reviews of that literature find it does not. What does help is matching the method to the material, such as using pictures to teach geography.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"Testing yourself on material produces better long-term retention than spending the same time re-reading it.","a":true,"why":"This is the testing effect, shown repeatedly since a 2006 study in which students who read a passage and then recalled it outperformed students who read it four times, when both groups were tested a week later. Retrieval appears to strengthen memory in a way that re-exposure does not.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"Spreading study sessions out over days produces better long-term recall than putting the same total hours into one sitting.","a":true,"why":"The spacing effect was first measured by Hermann Ebbinghaus on himself in the 1880s and has since been confirmed across ages, subjects and time-scales. Massed study feels more effective at the time, which is part of why people keep choosing it.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"The Stanford prison experiment is trusted because its results have been reproduced by other researchers.","a":false,"why":"It has not been successfully replicated, and recordings from the study itself show the guards were coached towards harshness rather than falling into it. It ran without a control condition on a dozen guards for six days, and is now taught as much for its methodological problems as for its conclusions.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"A famous 1957 experiment proved that split-second messages spliced into a cinema film made the audience buy more popcorn.","a":false,"why":"The market researcher James Vicary announced the result and never produced the data; in 1962 he conceded that the study was a stunt and the figures made up. Attempts to reproduce such an effect have found nothing of the kind, but the claim entered public belief and has never fully left it.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"A placebo can produce a measurable improvement even when the patient is told outright that it is a placebo.","a":true,"why":"Trials of so-called open-label placebos, beginning with a 2010 study of irritable bowel syndrome in which patients were given pills openly described as inert, have found improvements in reported symptoms against no-treatment controls. The effect is on how patients feel rather than on the underlying disease.","cat":"Psychology"},{"q":"Every even number greater than two has been proved to be the sum of two prime numbers.","a":false,"why":"This is Goldbach's conjecture, put in a letter to Euler in 1742, and it is still unproved. Computers have verified it for every even number up to about four quintillion, which is evidence and not a proof.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"There is no largest prime number, and this has been known since antiquity.","a":true,"why":"Euclid's Elements gives the proof: multiply any finite list of primes together and add one, and the result is divisible by none of them, so the list was incomplete. The argument is about two and a half thousand years old and has never needed improving.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"Fermat's Last Theorem went unproved for more than three centuries after it was written down.","a":true,"why":"Pierre de Fermat noted it in the margin of a book around 1637, claiming a proof he never set out. Andrew Wiles published a complete proof in 1995, after a first version presented in 1993 was found to have a gap that took a further year to close.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"A Möbius strip has only one side and one edge.","a":true,"why":"Give a paper band a half twist before joining the ends and a line drawn along the surface returns to its start having covered what were the front and the back. The same is true of the edge, which is a single closed curve.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"Zero was used as a number in Europe well before it reached India.","a":false,"why":"It went the other way. Zero as a number in its own right, with rules for calculating with it, was set out by Brahmagupta in India in 628 CE; it reached Europe through Arabic mathematics, and was still being resisted in some Italian cities in the thirteenth century.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"It has been proved that a circle cannot be squared with compass and straightedge.","a":true,"why":"The construction requires a length equal to the square root of pi, and compass-and-straightedge constructions can only produce certain algebraic numbers. Ferdinand von Lindemann proved in 1882 that pi is transcendental, which settled a problem the Greeks had left open.","cat":"Mathematics"},{"q":"The chemical symbols for iron, lead and gold are taken from their Latin names rather than their English ones.","a":true,"why":"Fe, Pb and Au come from ferrum, plumbum and aurum. Several other symbols work the same way, including Na for natrium, K for kalium, Ag for argentum and Sn for stannum.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Water can be made to boil at room temperature.","a":true,"why":"Boiling happens when a liquid's vapour pressure matches the pressure around it, so lowering the pressure lowers the boiling point. Pump a sealed flask down to about a fortieth of an atmosphere and water at 20 degrees Celsius boils vigorously while staying cool enough to touch.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Iron that has rusted weighs less than the iron it formed from.","a":false,"why":"It weighs more. Rust is iron combined with oxygen and water from its surroundings, so the oxide is heavier than the metal it replaced — which is exactly what Lavoisier's careful weighing of metals before and after burning established in the 1770s.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Glass loses quality each time it is recycled, so it can only go round a limited number of times.","a":false,"why":"Glass can be melted and re-formed indefinitely without degrading, which is unusual among common materials; paper fibres shorten and most plastics do lose properties with each cycle. The practical limits on glass recycling are collection and colour sorting rather than the material.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"Mendeleev left gaps in his periodic table for elements nobody had found, and predicted what they would be like.","a":true,"why":"His 1869 table left spaces below aluminium and silicon and set out expected atomic weights and properties for the missing elements. Gallium was isolated in 1875 and germanium in 1886, and both matched his predictions closely enough to make the table's authority.","cat":"Chemistry"},{"q":"The word 'vaccine' comes from the Latin for cow.","a":true,"why":"Edward Jenner's 1796 procedure used material from cowpox, vaccinia, to protect against smallpox, and vacca is Latin for cow. Pasteur later extended the word to inoculations against other diseases in Jenner's honour.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"Sugar makes children hyperactive.","a":false,"why":"Double-blind trials, in which neither the children nor the adults watching them know whether sugar was given, find no effect on behaviour. In one study parents told their child had been given sugar rated the child as more hyperactive even though the drink was sugar-free.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"Shaving makes hair grow back thicker and darker.","a":false,"why":"Shaving cuts the shaft above the skin and does not touch the follicle, which determines thickness and colour. Regrowth feels coarse because the blunt cut end is wider than a tapered tip, and looks darker because it is short and unbleached by sunlight.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"The active ingredient in aspirin has a natural relative in willow bark, which was used as a medicine for thousands of years before the drug existed.","a":true,"why":"Willow bark contains salicin, which the body converts to salicylic acid; Sumerian and Egyptian texts record willow preparations for pain and fever. Bayer's acetylation of salicylic acid in 1897 produced a compound gentler on the stomach, marketed as aspirin.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"Ignaz Semmelweis's evidence that handwashing cut deaths in a maternity ward was accepted by the medical profession as soon as he presented it.","a":false,"why":"His figures from Vienna in 1847 were striking — deaths on the ward he supervised fell from around 18 per cent to about 2 — and the profession largely rejected them, partly because he could offer no mechanism before germ theory. He was dismissed from his post and died in an asylum in 1865.","cat":"Medicine"},{"q":"The summit furthest from the centre of the Earth is not Mount Everest.","a":true,"why":"That distinction belongs to Chimborazo in Ecuador. The Earth bulges at the equator by about 21 kilometres, and Chimborazo sits close enough to it that its 6,263-metre summit stands about two kilometres further from the centre than Everest's 8,849-metre one.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"The Sahara has been green grassland with lakes and rivers within the span of human history.","a":true,"why":"Sediment cores, rock art and fossil pollen record an African Humid Period from roughly eleven thousand to five thousand years ago, when monsoon rains reached far north and the region held lakes, hippopotamus and cattle herders. The change was driven by a slow shift in the Earth's orbital tilt and precession.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"The Earth's magnetic field has reversed its polarity many times in the geological past.","a":true,"why":"Lavas lock in the field direction as they cool, and the sea floor either side of mid-ocean ridges carries a matching pattern of magnetic stripes recording reversal after reversal. The most recent full reversal was about 780,000 years ago.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"Alfred Wegener's proposal that the continents had drifted was accepted by geologists soon after he published it in 1912.","a":false,"why":"It was rejected for half a century, largely because he could not say what force would move a continent. Acceptance came in the 1960s with sea-floor spreading and the magnetic stripes on the ocean floor, which supplied the mechanism he had lacked.","cat":"Earth science"},{"q":"Nothing can travel faster than light in any medium.","a":false,"why":"Nothing outruns light in a vacuum, but light itself slows in matter, and charged particles routinely exceed that reduced speed. The blue glow around a submerged reactor core is Cherenkov radiation, produced by electrons moving through water faster than light does in water.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"A hammer and a feather dropped together on the Moon reach the ground at the same moment, and this has been tested there.","a":true,"why":"Commander David Scott dropped a geological hammer and a falcon feather at the end of the Apollo 15 moonwalk in 1971, on television, and they landed together. With no atmosphere there is no air resistance, so both fall at the same rate as Galileo had argued.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"The electron was identified before anyone knew that atoms have a nucleus.","a":true,"why":"J. J. Thomson identified the electron in 1897 from the deflection of cathode rays. The nucleus came fourteen years later, when Rutherford's team found that alpha particles fired at gold foil occasionally bounced back, which a diffuse atom could not explain.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"Absolute zero has been reached in the laboratory.","a":false,"why":"Experiments have come extraordinarily close — within a few hundred trillionths of a degree — but not to zero. The third law of thermodynamics implies it cannot be reached in a finite number of steps, since each stage of cooling removes only a fraction of the remaining energy.","cat":"Physics"},{"q":"The Moon is slowly moving away from the Earth.","a":true,"why":"Reflectors left on the surface by the Apollo missions let the distance be measured to within centimetres, and it is increasing by about 3.8 centimetres a year. Tides raised on the Earth drag ahead of the Moon and hand it energy, which lifts its orbit and lengthens our day.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Jupiter is more massive than every other planet in the solar system put together.","a":true,"why":"Jupiter is about 318 times the mass of the Earth, and roughly two and a half times the mass of the other seven planets combined. It is still around a thousandth of the mass of the Sun.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Halley's Comet was first recorded by Edmond Halley.","a":false,"why":"Chinese astronomers recorded it in 240 BCE and it appears in European and Islamic records for centuries afterwards, including the Bayeux Tapestry's account of 1066. Halley's contribution, in 1705, was to recognise that several such sightings were the same object returning and to predict its next appearance.","cat":"Astronomy"},{"q":"Humans are more closely related to mushrooms than mushrooms are to plants.","a":true,"why":"Fungi and animals share a more recent common ancestor with each other than either does with plants, and are grouped together as opisthokonts. Fungi also store energy as glycogen and build cell walls from chitin, as insects do, rather than from cellulose.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"A giraffe has the same number of neck vertebrae as a human being.","a":true,"why":"Almost all mammals have seven cervical vertebrae, however long or short the neck; the giraffe's are simply enormous. The rare exceptions are sloths and manatees.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"The blue whale is the largest living thing that has ever existed on Earth.","a":false,"why":"It is the largest animal, but a single honey fungus in Oregon's Blue Mountains, genetically one organism, spreads across about nine square kilometres of forest soil and is thought to weigh far more. Some clonal stands of aspen are contenders too.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Chameleons change colour chiefly to blend in with their surroundings.","a":false,"why":"Colour change is used mainly for signalling to other chameleons and for regulating temperature — darkening to absorb heat, lightening to reflect it. Their resting colours already suit their habitat, and the dramatic displays are shown to rivals and mates rather than to hide.","cat":"Biology"},{"q":"Julius Caesar was assassinated in the Senate House of Rome.","a":false,"why":"The Senate was meeting that day in a hall attached to the Theatre of Pompey, because the Curia in the Forum was being rebuilt after a fire. Plutarch notes the irony that Caesar fell at the foot of a statue of Pompey, the rival he had defeated.","cat":"Ancient & classical"},{"q":"Marco Polo brought pasta to Italy from China.","a":false,"why":"Pasta is documented in Italy before he returned in 1295 — a Sicilian account of the 1150s describes a dried strand pasta being made near Palermo and exported. The Marco Polo story appears to originate in an American trade magazine article of the 1920s.","cat":"Medieval & early modern"},{"q":"The Wright brothers' first powered flight in 1903 covered less ground than the wingspan of a modern jumbo jet.","a":true,"why":"The first of the four flights that day ran 120 feet in twelve seconds; a Boeing 747's wingspan is over 200 feet. The fourth flight of the same morning managed 852 feet before the aircraft was damaged on landing.","cat":"Modern history"},{"q":"The first person to see bacteria did so through lenses he had ground himself.","a":true,"why":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was a Delft draper with no university training who made his own single-lens microscopes, and in the 1670s reported the 'animalcules' in pond water, dental plaque and other samples to the Royal Society. His lens-making method was good enough that nobody matched his images for over a century.","cat":"Science & invention"}];
